Buffer overflow.
A **buffer overflow** writes more data into a buffer than it can hold, overwriting neighbouring memory. On the stack it can overwrite the saved **return address**, so the function returns into attacker code.

void login(char *in) {
    char buf[8];
    strcpy(buf, in);   /* no length check */
}

• Typical of C and C++, where arrays have no automatic bounds check.
• **Defences**: bounds-checked functions (strncpy, fgets, snprintf), memory-safe languages, **stack canaries**, **DEP/NX** (stack not executable), **ASLR** (random addresses), fuzz testing.

Example: Passing a 100-character string to login() writes 92 bytes past buf, which can replace the return address with the address of injected code.

